.TH CJSON_SERIALIZE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_serialize \- serialize a DOM tree to a compact JSON string
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cjson.h>

char *cjson_serialize(cjson node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Walks the DOM tree rooted at node and produces a compact (no whitespace)
JSON string.  A NULL node produces the string "null".

Doubles are serialized with the shortest round-trip decimal
representation (up to 17 significant digits), always using '.' as the
decimal separator per RFC 8259, regardless of the calling thread's
ambient LC_NUMERIC locale setting.  Integers use %lld.  Non-finite
doubles (Inf, NaN) are serialized as "null".

A subtree nested more than 500 levels deep (arrays and/or objects, in
any combination) is not serialized further; NULL is returned instead.
A tree built directly through the construction API (cjson_list_push(),
cjson_dictionary_set()) is not otherwise bounded in depth the way a
parsed document is, so this cap applies independently of cjson_parse().
Ordinary trees never approach this depth.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Heap-allocated null-terminated string on success.
NULL on allocation failure, or if node is nested more than 500 levels
deep.
The caller must free the string with cjson_serialize_free().
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_serialize_pretty (3),
.BR cjson_serialize_free (3),
.BR cjson_parse (3)
